var DAVID_INDEX = [{"title": "Getting started", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#getting-started", "text": "David has three kinds of mods. Pick the simplest one that can do the job: Tier What it is Good for Shader One .glsl file Per-pixel effects that run on the GPU: color changes, patterns, distortions Lua One or more .lua files Effects, commands that edit layers, painting tools Native A DLL or .so built from C or C++ Heavy effects and new file formats"}, {"title": "Where mods live", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#where-mods-live", "text": "Every mod is a folder with a mod.json inside. David looks in two places: the mods folder next to the program (mods that ship with David, and mods/examples/ ); the mods folder in your config folder: %APPDATA%\\David\\mods on Windows, ~/.config/david/mods on Linux. Mods > Open mods folder opens it. A mod in your config folder replaces a bundled mod with the same folder name."}, {"title": "mod.json", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#mod-json", "text": "{ \"name\": \"Scanlines\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"api\": 1, \"kind\": \"shader\", \"entry\": \"scanlines.glsl\", \"description\": \"Darkens every other group of rows.\" } Field Meaning name Shown in Settings > Mods. version Your mod's version, any text. api The David API version the mod needs. This David has version 1; a higher number refuses to load. kind shader , lua or native . entry The file David loads: the .gl"}, {"title": "Turning mods on and seeing errors", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#turning-mods-on-and-seeing-errors", "text": "Settings > Mods (or Mods > Manage mods ) lists every mod with a switch. Your own mods start on; the examples start off. A mod that fails to load shows its error in red under its name: a JSON mistake, a Lua syntax error with its line, or a GLSL compile error. What david.log and print write shows under Log . Settings > Mods with the example mods and a broken one"}, {"title": "The edit loop", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#the-edit-loop", "text": "Shader and Lua mods reload by themselves: keep David open, save your file, and within a second the new version is in the menus. If an effect's dialog is open, the reload waits until you close it. Native mods and new Lua tools need a restart. The same mods run from the command line, which is handy for quick checks: david-cli --mod scanlines in.png --effect scanlines:spacing=4 -o out.png david-cli -"}, {"title": "Next", "page": "Getting started", "url": "index.html#next", "text": "Shader effect: Scanlines Lua command: Palette Swap Lua tool: Checker Brush Lua windows: Canvas Info Native plugin: a QOI-like file type API reference"}, {"title": "Getting started", "page": "Page", "url": "index.html", "text": "Getting started David has three kinds of mods. Pick the simplest one that can do the job: Tier What it is Good for Shader One .glsl file Per-pixel effects that run on the GPU: color changes, patterns, distortions Lua One or more .lua files Effects, commands that edit layers, painting tools Native A "}, {"title": "Tutorial: Scanlines (shader effect)", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#tutorial-scanlines-shader-effect", "text": "We'll write an effect that darkens rows like an old CRT screen, with four options: spacing, thickness, darkness and the line color. The finished mod is mods/examples/scanlines ."}, {"title": "1. The folder", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#1-the-folder", "text": "Make a folder scanlines in your mods folder with this mod.json : { \"name\": \"Scanlines\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"api\": 1, \"kind\": \"shader\", \"entry\": \"scanlines.glsl\" }"}, {"title": "2. The effect function", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#2-the-effect-function", "text": "A shader mod defines one function, vec4 effect(vec2 pos) . David calls it for every pixel of the selection (or the whole layer). pos is the pixel's position in the image, and pixel(pos) reads the image there. Colors are straight RGBA from 0 to 1. vec4 effect(vec2 pos) { return pixel(pos); } That effect does nothing yet. Save it as scanlines.glsl , turn the mod on in Settings > Mods, and it shows u"}, {"title": "3. Name and options", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#3-name-and-options", "text": "Comment lines that start with // @ describe the effect. Each @param becomes a uniform of the same name and a control in the dialog: // @name Scanlines // @id scanlines // @category Effects/Stylize // @param spacing int 2..16 = 2 \"Spacing\" // @param thickness int 1..8 = 1 \"Thickness\" // @param darkness float 0..1 = 0.5 \"Darkness\" // @param tint color = #000000FF \"Line color\" Type Line Uniform float"}, {"title": "4. The lines", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#4-the-lines", "text": "vec4 effect(vec2 pos) { vec4 c = pixel(pos); bool line = mod(pos.y, float(spacing)) < float(thickness); if(line) c.rgb = mix(c.rgb, tint.rgb, darkness * tint.a); return c; } Save. The mod reloads, and Effects > Stylize > Scanlines opens the dialog with a live preview. OK makes it one undo step; Ctrl+F repeats it. The Scanlines dialog over its live preview"}, {"title": "What else", "page": "Shader effect", "url": "tutorial-shader.html#what-else", "text": "The uniforms uSize (the area's size) and uOrigin (its top-left in the image) are there if you need them. pixel() clamps at the edge of the area. The area is the selection's bounding box. A GLSL error shows in Settings > Mods with its line number, counted from the top of your file. The CLI runs shader mods too; it opens a hidden window for the GPU."}, {"title": "Shader effect", "page": "Page", "url": "tutorial-shader.html", "text": "Tutorial: Scanlines (shader effect) We'll write an effect that darkens rows like an old CRT screen, with four options: spacing, thickness, darkness and the line color. The finished mod is mods/examples/scanlines . 1. The folder Make a folder scanlines in your mods folder with this mod.json : { \"name"}, {"title": "Tutorial: Palette Swap (Lua command)", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#tutorial-palette-swap-lua-command", "text": "A command that replaces one color with another in the active layer, inside the selection. It asks for the two colors and a tolerance first, and the whole change is one undo step. The finished mod is mods/examples/palette-swap ."}, {"title": "1. mod.json", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#1-mod-json", "text": "{ \"name\": \"Palette Swap\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"api\": 1, \"kind\": \"lua\", \"entry\": \"main.lua\" }"}, {"title": "2. Registering the command", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#2-registering-the-command", "text": "main.lua runs once when the mod loads. It registers things through the david table: david.command{ id = \"palette-swap\", name = \"Palette Swap\", menu = \"Mods/Color\", params = { { id = \"from\", label = \"Replace\", type = \"color\" }, { id = \"to\", label = \"With\", type = \"color\" }, { id = \"tolerance\", label = \"Tolerance\", type = \"int\", min = 0, max = 255, default = 0 }, }, run = function(doc, p) -- the edi"}, {"title": "3. Editing pixels", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#3-editing-pixels", "text": "doc:layer() is the active layer. Colors are plain integers: david.channels(c) splits one into r, g, b, a (0–255), and david.rgba(r, g, b, a) packs them. run = function(doc, p) local layer = doc:layer() local fr, fg, fb = david.channels(p.from) local tr, tg, tb = david.channels(p.to) for y = 0, doc.height - 1 do for x = 0, doc.width - 1 do local r, g, b, a = david.channels(layer:get(x, y)) local cl"}, {"title": "4. Undo", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#4-undo", "text": "You don't do anything for undo: every pixel and layer change inside run becomes one History step named after the command. If run raises an error, everything it changed is put back and the message shows in a dialog."}, {"title": "Notes", "page": "Lua command", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html#notes", "text": "A call that runs longer than 10 seconds is stopped, so a mistake can't freeze David. Big images: reading and writing pixels one by one from Lua is slow past a few megapixels. An effect ( david.effect ) runs only over the selection's bounds; prefer it for per-pixel work. The same command logic can run from the CLI as a script: see the cookbook ."}, {"title": "Lua command", "page": "Page", "url": "tutorial-lua-command.html", "text": "Tutorial: Palette Swap (Lua command) A command that replaces one color with another in the active layer, inside the selection. It asks for the two colors and a tolerance first, and the whole change is one undo step. The finished mod is mods/examples/palette-swap . 1. mod.json { \"name\": \"Palette Swap"}, {"title": "Tutorial: Checker Brush (Lua tool)", "page": "Lua tool", "url": "tutorial-lua-tool.html#tutorial-checker-brush-lua-tool", "text": "A painting tool that only paints every other pixel, in a checkerboard. The finished mod is mods/examples/checker-brush ."}, {"title": "How tools are built", "page": "Lua tool", "url": "tutorial-lua-tool.html#how-tools-are-built", "text": "A David tool has three parts: the controller (how pointer input becomes positions: freehand here), the point shape (the round brush, sized by the tool bar's width) and the ink (what a covered pixel becomes). A Lua tool keeps the Paintbrush's controller and point shape and gives its own ink."}, {"title": "The code", "page": "Lua tool", "url": "tutorial-lua-tool.html#the-code", "text": "mod.json is the same as in the command tutorial , with \"entry\": \"main.lua\" . Then: david.tool{ id = \"checker-brush\", name = \"Checker Brush\", ink = function(x, y, before, color) if (x + y) % 2 == 0 then return color end -- returning nothing leaves the pixel as it was end, } ink(x, y, before, color) gets the pixel's position, its color before the stroke and the current color (primary for the left bu"}, {"title": "Ideas", "page": "Lua tool", "url": "tutorial-lua-tool.html#ideas", "text": "Shading: return a lighter or darker version of before . Patterns: use x % 4 and y % 4 to stamp a texture. Symmetry and tiled mode work with mod tools too: they happen before the ink is called."}, {"title": "Lua tool", "page": "Page", "url": "tutorial-lua-tool.html", "text": "Tutorial: Checker Brush (Lua tool) A painting tool that only paints every other pixel, in a checkerboard. The finished mod is mods/examples/checker-brush . How tools are built A David tool has three parts: the controller (how pointer input becomes positions: freehand here), the point shape (the roun"}, {"title": "Tutorial: Canvas Info (Lua windows)", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#tutorial-canvas-info-lua-windows", "text": "A tool window that docks next to Layers and shows the image's size, layers and frames, plus a dialog it opens to rename the layers. The finished mod is mods/examples/canvas-info ."}, {"title": "Two kinds of window", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#two-kinds-of-window", "text": "Tool window ( kind = \"tool\" , the default): docks like Colors, Layers and History. It first opens as a tab next to Layers; drag its tab to dock it anywhere else. With Settings > Appearance > floating windows on, it floats like the other windows. Dialog ( kind = \"dialog\" ): floats over the canvas like Settings and never docks. Esc closes it. Both are listed in the Mods menu (checked while open), an"}, {"title": "The tool window", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#the-tool-window", "text": "mod.json is the same as in the command tutorial , with \"entry\": \"main.lua\" . Then: local count = 0 david.window{ id = \"canvas-info\", title = \"Canvas Info\", draw = function(ui, doc) ui:header(\"Image\") ui:text(doc.width .. \" x \" .. doc.height .. \" px\") ui:text(doc:layerCount() .. \" layers, \" .. doc:frameCount() .. \" frames\") ui:header(\"Active layer\") local layer = doc:layer() ui:text(layer.name) if "}, {"title": "The dialog", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#the-dialog", "text": "local prefix, numbered, start = \"Layer\", true, 1 david.window{ id = \"rename\", title = \"Rename Layers\", kind = \"dialog\", width = 360, height = 200, draw = function(ui, doc) prefix = ui:input(\"Prefix\", prefix) numbered = ui:checkbox(\"Number them\", numbered) if numbered then start = ui:slider(\"Start at\", start, 0, 100) end ui:separator() if ui:button(\"Apply\", 100) then for i = 1, doc:layerCount() do "}, {"title": "Undo", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#undo", "text": "Everything a draw call changes in the document is one undo step , named after the window (here \"Rename Layers\"). So change the document on a button press, not while a slider moves, or every frame of the drag becomes its own step."}, {"title": "When it goes wrong", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#when-it-goes-wrong", "text": "If draw raises an error, the window shows the error instead of its contents and the error goes to the mod's log (Settings > Mods). Fix the file and save it: the mod reloads and the window draws again."}, {"title": "Widgets", "page": "Lua windows", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html#widgets", "text": "Call Returns ui:text(s) , ui:muted(s) , ui:header(s) ui:separator() , ui:spacing() , ui:sameLine() ui:button(label [, width]) pressed ui:checkbox(label, value) value, changed ui:slider(label, value, min, max [, step]) value, changed ui:choice(label, index, { names }) index (from 1), changed ui:color(label, color) color, changed ui:input(label, text) text, changed ui:width() the width left on the r"}, {"title": "Lua windows", "page": "Page", "url": "tutorial-lua-window.html", "text": "Tutorial: Canvas Info (Lua windows) A tool window that docks next to Layers and shows the image's size, layers and frames, plus a dialog it opens to rename the layers. The finished mod is mods/examples/canvas-info . Two kinds of window Tool window ( kind = \"tool\" , the default): docks like Colors, L"}, {"title": "Tutorial: a QOI-like file type (native plugin)", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#tutorial-a-qoi-like-file-type-native-plugin", "text": "A plugin in C that opens and saves .qlike files (runs of RGBA pixels), and adds a small Swap Red/Blue effect. The finished mod is mods/examples/qoi-like ; David's own build compiles it too."}, {"title": "The C ABI", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#the-c-abi", "text": "Native plugins talk to David through one C header, sdk/david_plugin.h . It is plain C, so a plugin can be built with any compiler (MSVC, GCC, Clang) and any language that can export a C function. The plugin exports one function: DAVID_PLUGIN_EXPORT int david_plugin_init(const david_host* host); host has the API version, an allocator, register_effect , register_format and log . Return 0 when everyt"}, {"title": "1. mod.json", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#1-mod-json", "text": "{ \"name\": \"QOI-like File Type\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"api\": 1, \"kind\": \"native\", \"entry\": \"qlike\" } entry is the library name without its extension: David loads qlike.dll on Windows and libqlike.so or qlike.so on Linux."}, {"title": "2. The format", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#2-the-format", "text": "static const david_host* s_host; static int load(void* user, const uint8_t* bytes, size_t size, david_image* out) { if(size < 12 || memcmp(bytes, \"QLIK\", 4)) return 1; /* read width and height, then: */ out->pixels = (uint32_t*)s_host->alloc((size_t)w * h * 4); /* fill out->pixels, set out->width and out->height */ return 0; } static int save(void* user, const david_image* img, uint8_t** bytes, si"}, {"title": "3. Registering", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#3-registering", "text": "DAVID_PLUGIN_EXPORT int david_plugin_init(const david_host* host) { if(host->api_version < DAVID_API_VERSION) return 1; s_host = host; david_format format = { \"qlike\", \"QOI-like\", load, save, NULL }; david_effect effect = { \"swap-red-blue\", \"Swap Red/Blue\", \"Effects/Mods\", \"[]\", swapRedBlue, NULL }; if(host->register_format(&format)) return 2; if(host->register_effect(&effect)) return 3; return 0;"}, {"title": "4. Building", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#4-building", "text": "The example has its own CMakeLists.txt : cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20) project(qoi_like C) set(DAVID_SDK \"${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../../../sdk\" CACHE PATH \"Folder with david_plugin.h\") add_library(qlike SHARED qoi_like.c) target_include_directories(qlike PRIVATE ${DAVID_SDK}) set_target_properties(qlike PROPERTIES PREFIX \"\") Windows (a Visual Studio developer prompt) and Linux are the s"}, {"title": "Safety", "page": "Native plugin", "url": "tutorial-native.html#safety", "text": "A native plugin runs with David's full rights: only install plugins you trust. Lua and shader mods are sandboxed; native ones can't be."}, {"title": "Native plugin", "page": "Page", "url": "tutorial-native.html", "text": "Tutorial: a QOI-like file type (native plugin) A plugin in C that opens and saves .qlike files (runs of RGBA pixels), and adds a small Swap Red/Blue effect. The finished mod is mods/examples/qoi-like ; David's own build compiles it too. The C ABI Native plugins talk to David through one C header, sd"}, {"title": "Concepts", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#concepts", "text": ""}, {"title": "Pixels and coordinates", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#pixels-and-coordinates", "text": "Coordinates are image pixels from the top-left corner, starting at 0. x grows to the right, y down. A color is one 32-bit integer: straight (not premultiplied) RGBA, red in the low byte. In Lua use david.rgba and david.channels instead of doing the bit math. Layers are numbered from 1 (the bottom layer) in Lua, the usual Lua way."}, {"title": "Tiles", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#tiles", "text": "An image is stored in tiles of 256 × 256 pixels, and an empty tile takes no memory. That's why a fresh 8000 × 8000 layer costs nothing until you paint on it. For mods it is invisible: get and set work anywhere in the image."}, {"title": "The selection", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#the-selection", "text": "The selection is a mask with a value from 0 to 255 per pixel, not a shape: soft edges (from the magic wand or a feathered lasso) are partly selected pixels. Effects only change pixels inside the selection, blended by the mask: a pixel selected at 128 gets half the effect. You don't do anything for that. An effect's image is the selection's bounding box plus the effect's margin. Outside the boundin"}, {"title": "Undo transactions", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#undo-transactions", "text": "Each effect run, command run or tool stroke is one History step. David records the tiles a step touches (before and after), so undo memory stays small even on big images. Inside a Lua command every change goes into the same step, in order, and a Lua error undoes everything the command did."}, {"title": "Color spaces", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#color-spaces", "text": "Colors are 8-bit sRGB. A few things work in other spaces: Linear light for resampling (Image > Resize, with its gamma option on). OKLab , a perceptual space, for the magic wand's and bucket's tolerance, gradients, palette matching in Dither, and Hue / Saturation. Mods can do their own conversions; the formulas are in Björn Ottosson's OKLab article ."}, {"title": "The sandbox", "page": "Concepts", "url": "concepts.html#the-sandbox", "text": "Lua mods run with only math , string , table , utf8 and the safe parts of the base library. There is no os , io , package , require , load , loadfile or dofile , and compiled bytecode can't be loaded. A Lua mod can only change the image it is given. Each call has a 10-second limit. Shader mods only see the area they run on. Native plugins are not sandboxed (see the native tutorial )."}, {"title": "Concepts", "page": "Page", "url": "concepts.html", "text": "Concepts Pixels and coordinates Coordinates are image pixels from the top-left corner, starting at 0. x grows to the right, y down. A color is one 32-bit integer: straight (not premultiplied) RGBA, red in the low byte. In Lua use david.rgba and david.channels instead of doing the bit math. Layers ar"}, {"title": "Cookbook", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#cookbook", "text": ""}, {"title": "Read every pixel in the selection (Lua command)", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#read-every-pixel-in-the-selection-lua-command", "text": "run = function(doc) local x0, y0, w, h = doc:selectionBounds() if not x0 then x0, y0, w, h = 0, 0, doc.width, doc.height end local layer = doc:layer() for y = y0, y0 + h - 1 do for x = x0, x0 + w - 1 do if doc:selected(x, y) > 0 then local r, g, b, a = david.channels(layer:get(x, y)) -- ... end end end end"}, {"title": "An effect with a margin (Lua)", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#an-effect-with-a-margin-lua", "text": "An effect that reads neighbors needs pixels around the selection. Give it a margin, and img:get can read that far out: david.effect{ id = \"box-blur-3\", name = \"Box Blur 3×3\", margin = 1, run = function(img, p) for y = 0, img.height - 1 do for x = 0, img.width - 1 do local sr, sg, sb, sa = 0, 0, 0, 0 for dy = -1, 1 do for dx = -1, 1 do local r, g, b, a = david.channels(img:get(x + dx, y + dy)) sr, "}, {"title": "Add a layer and fill it", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#add-a-layer-and-fill-it", "text": "david.command{ id = \"red-layer\", name = \"Red Layer\", run = function(doc) local layer = doc:addLayer(\"Red\") layer.blend = \"multiply\" layer.opacity = 128 for y = 0, doc.height - 1 do for x = 0, doc.width - 1 do layer:set(x, y, david.rgba(255, 0, 0)) end end end }"}, {"title": "Run a built-in effect from a command", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#run-a-built-in-effect-from-a-command", "text": "run = function(doc) doc:applyEffect(\"gaussian-blur\", { radius = 4 }) doc:applyEffect(\"posterize\", { levels = 6 }) end"}, {"title": "Do something on every frame (Lua command)", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#do-something-on-every-frame-lua-command", "text": "A document can be an animation. Layers read and write the active frame, so a command loops over the frames by moving doc.activeFrame ; it's still one undo step. david.command{ id = \"flash\", name = \"Flash Every Other Frame\", run = function(doc) local layer = doc:layer() for f = 2, doc:frameCount(), 2 do doc.activeFrame = f for y = 0, doc.height - 1 do for x = 0, doc.width - 1 do local r, g, b, a = "}, {"title": "Batch-process a folder from the CLI", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#batch-process-a-folder-from-the-cli", "text": "david-cli --batch \"sprites/*.png\" --resize 300% --filter nearest --effect outline:color=#000 --out-dir big/ Every match goes to big/ with the same name. --format webp changes the format."}, {"title": "A script on one image", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#a-script-on-one-image", "text": "--script runs a Lua file on the image, with david.image as the document and --arg values in david.args : -- tint.lua: david-cli in.png --script tint.lua --arg amount=40 -o out.png local doc = david.image local amount = tonumber(david.args.amount or \"20\") local layer = doc:layer() for y = 0, doc.height - 1 do for x = 0, doc.width - 1 do local r, g, b, a = david.channels(layer:get(x, y)) layer:set(x"}, {"title": "Pipe images", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#pipe-images", "text": "- reads stdin or writes stdout (PNG unless --format says otherwise): cat in.png | david-cli - --effect invert -o - > out.png"}, {"title": "Find an effect's parameters", "page": "Cookbook", "url": "cookbook.html#find-an-effect-s-parameters", "text": "david-cli --list-effects david-cli --list-effects --json"}, {"title": "Cookbook", "page": "Page", "url": "cookbook.html", "text": "Cookbook Read every pixel in the selection (Lua command) run = function(doc) local x0, y0, w, h = doc:selectionBounds() if not x0 then x0, y0, w, h = 0, 0, doc.width, doc.height end local layer = doc:layer() for y = y0, y0 + h - 1 do for x = x0, x0 + w - 1 do if doc:selected(x, y) > 0 then local r, "}, {"title": "Lua: david", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#lua-david", "text": "david.apiVersion david.apiVersion -> integer The API version (1). mod.json's \"api\" must not be higher. david.rgba david.rgba(r, g, b [, a]) -> color Packs 0-255 channels into a color. Alpha defaults to 255. Colors are plain integers. local red = david.rgba(255, 0, 0) david.channels david.channels(color) -> r, g, b, a Unpacks a color into 0-255 channels. local r, g, b, a = david.channels(img:get(x,"}, {"title": "Lua: Image", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#lua-image", "text": "Image.width img.width -> integer Width of the area the effect runs on (the selection's bounds plus the effect's margin). Image.height img.height -> integer Height of the area. Image.x img.x, img.y -> integer Image position of the area's top-left pixel, for patterns that must line up across the image. Image:get img:get(x, y) -> color The pixel before the effect (0 outside the area). Coordinates are"}, {"title": "Lua: Document", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#lua-document", "text": "Document.width doc.width, doc.height -> integer The image size in pixels. Document.primary doc.primary, doc.secondary -> color The primary and secondary colors. Document.palette doc.palette -> { color, ... } The current palette, in order (a copy; changing the table changes nothing). Document.activeLayer doc.activeLayer -> integer Index of the active layer; 1 is the bottom layer. Document:layerCoun"}, {"title": "Lua: Layer", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#lua-layer", "text": "Layer.name layer.name -> string (read and write) The layer's name. Changing it is part of the command's undo step. Layer.visible layer.visible -> boolean (read and write) Layer.opacity layer.opacity -> integer 0-255 (read and write) Layer.blend layer.blend -> string (read and write) The blend mode id: normal, multiply, additive, color-burn, color-dodge, reflect, glow, overlay, difference, negation"}, {"title": "Lua: Ui", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#lua-ui", "text": "Ui:text ui:text(text) / ui:muted(text) A line of text; muted is the dimmer color of hints. ui:text(\"Layers: \" .. doc:layerCount()) Ui:header ui:header(text) A section header (upper case, muted), like the ones in Settings. Ui:separator ui:separator() / ui:spacing() / ui:sameLine() A line across the window, a little vertical space, or the next widget on the same row as the last one. Ui:button ui:but"}, {"title": "C ABI (native plugins)", "page": "API reference", "url": "reference.html#c-abi", "text": "From sdk/david_plugin.h . Pixels are straight RGBA8, red in the low byte, rows top first. DAVID_API_VERSION #define DAVID_API_VERSION 1 The API version this header describes. david_plugin_init gets the host's version; return 0 to refuse an older host. david_image typedef struct { int width, height; uint32_t* pixels; } david_image An RGBA8 image: width * height pixels, straight alpha, red in the lo"}, {"title": "API reference", "page": "Page", "url": "reference.html", "text": "API reference Generated from the annotations in scripting/LuaApi.cpp and sdk/david_plugin.h , so it always matches the code. Colors are integers (use david.rgba and david.channels ); layer indices start at 1. Lua: david david.apiVersion david.apiVersion -> integer The API version (1). mod.json's \"ap"}, {"title": "Built-in effects", "page": "Page", "url": "effects.html", "text": "Built-in effects Every effect and adjustment, with the ids the CLI, Lua ( doc:applyEffect ) and the dialogs share. Colors are written #rrggbb or #rgb ; a choice takes its name or index; a curve is x:y/x:y per channel, channels split by | . david-cli in.png --effect gaussian-blur:radius=3 --effect ou"}, {"title": "Pattern, theme and file formats", "page": "Patterns, themes, files", "url": "formats.html#pattern-theme-and-file-formats", "text": "These aren't code, but they are ways to extend David."}, {"title": "Dither patterns", "page": "Patterns, themes, files", "url": "formats.html#dither-patterns", "text": "Effects > Color > Dither with the method Pattern uses a threshold matrix. David ships Bayer 2×2, 4×4, 8×8 and blue noise, and loads more from the patterns folder next to the program and in your config folder. A pattern is referred to by its id, also from the CLI: --effect dither:method=Pattern,pattern=my-pattern . File Id Format .png the file name A grayscale image; darker means a lower threshold."}, {"title": "Themes", "page": "Patterns, themes, files", "url": "formats.html#themes", "text": "A theme is one JSON file in the themes folder (built in) or your config folder's themes (yours). Settings > Appearance makes a copy for you to edit, and David reloads it when the file changes. { \"name\": \"Dev\", \"colors\": { \"bg\": \"#1A1A1A\", \"surface\": \"#212121\", \"accent\": \"#8BE9FD\", \"...\": \"...\" }, \"shape\": { \"radius\": 4, \"borderWidth\": 0, \"spacing\": 6, \"fontSize\": 15, \"font\": \"\" } } Token Used for "}, {"title": "The .david file", "page": "Patterns, themes, files", "url": "formats.html#the-david-file", "text": "A zip: doc.json , layers/0.png , layers/1.png , ... (bottom first) and merged.png (the flattened image, for previews). { \"app\": \"david\", \"version\": 1, \"width\": 64, \"height\": 64, \"dpi\": 96, \"active\": 1, \"palette\": [\"1a1c2c\", \"5d275d\"], \"layers\": [ { \"name\": \"Background\", \"visible\": true, \"opacity\": 255, \"blend\": \"normal\", \"file\": \"layers/0.png\" } ] } David also reads and writes OpenRaster ( .ora ),"}, {"title": "Patterns, themes, files", "page": "Page", "url": "formats.html", "text": "Pattern, theme and file formats These aren't code, but they are ways to extend David. Dither patterns Effects > Color > Dither with the method Pattern uses a threshold matrix. David ships Bayer 2×2, 4×4, 8×8 and blue noise, and loads more from the patterns folder next to the program and in your conf"}, {"title": "Versioning", "page": "Versioning", "url": "versioning.html#versioning", "text": ""}, {"title": "The API version", "page": "Versioning", "url": "versioning.html#the-api-version", "text": "David has one API version for all three tiers. It is 1 . mod.json 's \"api\" says the version a mod needs. David refuses mods that need a newer version than its own, with a clear error. Lua mods can read david.apiVersion ; native plugins get host->api_version ."}, {"title": "The policy", "page": "Versioning", "url": "versioning.html#the-policy", "text": "New functions, fields and parameters can arrive in the same version. Mods should ignore what they don't know. Removing or changing anything raises the version. A deprecated feature keeps working for at least one more version and is listed below first. Effect ids ( gaussian-blur , dither , ...) and their parameter ids are part of the API, because CLI scripts use them. The native ABI only grows at t"}, {"title": "Changes", "page": "Versioning", "url": "versioning.html#changes", "text": "Version Changes 1 First version: shader effects with @param , the Lua david API (effects, commands, tools, documents, layers), david_plugin.h (effects, file formats), CLI --effect , --script . 1 (added) Animation frames: doc:frameCount() , doc.activeFrame , doc.fps , doc:frameDuration() / setFrameDuration() , doc:addFrame() , doc:deleteFrame() , doc.tags . Layers read and write the active frame, s"}, {"title": "Deprecated", "page": "Versioning", "url": "versioning.html#deprecated", "text": "Nothing yet."}, {"title": "Versioning", "page": "Page", "url": "versioning.html", "text": "Versioning The API version David has one API version for all three tiers. It is 1 . mod.json 's \"api\" says the version a mod needs. David refuses mods that need a newer version than its own, with a clear error. Lua mods can read david.apiVersion ; native plugins get host->api_version . The policy Ne"}];
